<section id="journey" class="section active"><div class="hero"><div class="ey">START HERE</div><h2>This is your treatment journey — not just a report.</h2><p class="muted">Every step answers one patient question: what happened, what your reports show, what each medicine is doing inside your body, what side effects arise from the same mechanism, what ILMB can change by another route, and what we must measure to know whether it worked.</p></div><div class="journey"><div class="step" data-go="reports"><b>01</b><strong>Read my reports</strong><span>See the MRI, MRA, CT and lab story in plain English.</span></div><div class="step" data-go="event"><b>02</b><strong>Understand the stroke</strong><span>Right MCA flow loss → right-brain injury → left-sided weakness.</span></div><div class="step" data-go="meds"><b>03</b><strong>Compare every medicine</strong><span>Desired effect → how it works → side effects → ILMB target.</span></div><div class="step" data-go="paths"><b>04</b><strong>See the two paths</strong><span>Pharma and ILMB compared against the same required body output.</span></div><div class="step" data-go="ilmb"><b>05</b><strong>Follow my ILMB treatment</strong><span>What happens next and how response will be measured.</span></div><div class="step" data-go="labs"><b>06</b><strong>Measure the result</strong><span>LOINC keeps each test identity exact.</span></div><div class="step" data-go="evidence"><b>07</b><strong>See the science layer</strong><span>ChEBI + Reactome connect chemicals to pathways behind the scenes.</span></div><div class="step" data-go="journey"><b>08</b><strong>Review again</strong><span>KEEP · MODIFY · INVESTIGATE · ESCALATE.</span></div></div><div class="grid g4" style="margin-top:14px"><div class="card kpi"><div class="muted small">Triglycerides</div><div class="v">301</div><span class="badge bad">mg/dL · high</span></div><div class="card kpi"><div class="muted small">25-OH Vitamin D</div><div class="v">13.9</div><span class="badge bad">ng/mL · low</span></div><div class="card kpi"><div class="muted small">Platelets</div><div class="v">133</div><span class="badge watch">×10³/µL</span></div><div class="card kpi"><div class="muted small">HbA1c</div><div class="v">5.8%</div><span class="badge watch">context</span></div></div></section>
<section id="reports" class="section"><div class="hero"><div class="ey">MY REPORTS</div><h2>What the records actually say.</h2><p class="muted">The report layer comes first. Treatment is interpreted against what is known, what has recovered, and what is still unknown.</p></div><div style="margin-top:14px"><div class="report-card"><div><b>23 Aug 2024</b><div class="small muted">MRI brain</div></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>Multiple fresh non-haemorrhagic right fronto-parieto-temporal infarcts.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning for Nilesh</strong><p>Several right-brain regions were injured because their blood supply failed.</p></div></div><div class="report-card"><div><b>23 Aug 2024</b><div class="small muted">MRA brain</div></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>High-grade right MCA occlusion from origin with severe paucity of distal branches.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>The main artery supplying a large right-brain territory was severely compromised.</p></div></div><div class="report-card"><div><b>21 Jul 2025</b><div class="small muted">MRI / MRA</div></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>Right MCA M1 still showed no flow; faint M2 enhancement questioned as collateral.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>Severe vessel abnormality persisted. Alternative collateral supply may exist but was not quantified.</p></div></div><div class="report-card"><div><b>26 Jul 2025</b><div class="small muted">Clinical follow-up</div></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>Normal speech; no recorded deficit.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>Function recovered much more than the vessel image alone suggests, raising the importance of collateral flow and neuroplasticity.</p></div></div><div class="report-card"><div><b>05 May 2026</b><div class="small muted">Laboratory panel</div></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>TG 301, ALT 81, creatinine 1.27, eGFR 72, K 5.3, platelets 133k, vitamin D 13.9, TSH 5.386, HbA1c 5.8.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>Vascular recurrence, treatment safety, metabolic state and nutrient state need to be tracked together.</p></div></div><div class="report-card"><div><b>31 Jul 2026</b><div class="small muted">Plain CT brain</div></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>Old right-sided infarcts; no acute infarct, haemorrhage or mass.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>No new acute lesion was seen, but plain CT does not quantify current MCA lumen, collateral reserve or tissue perfusion.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section id="event" class="section"><div class="grid g2"><div class="card"><div class="ey">WHAT HAPPENED</div><h2>The stroke chain</h2><div class="mini-flow"><span class="node">Right MCA flow ↓</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">O₂ / glucose delivery ↓</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">ATP failure</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">brain tissue injury</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">left weakness / numbness</span></div><p class="muted small">The initial recorded power was 3/5 on the left side.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="ey">WHY RECOVERY MATTERS</div><h2>Structure and function diverged</h2><p>The artery remained severely abnormal on later imaging, yet later clinical notes recorded no deficit.</p><div class="call">That means the key questions are current perfusion, collateral circulation and how the motor network adapted — not only whether the artery is open.</div></div></div></section>
<section id="body" class="section"><div class="card"><div class="ey">EXPLORE MY BODY</div><h2>Tap a system</h2><div class="systems"><button class="system" onclick="openSys('brain')"><b>Brain / right MCA</b><span>Perfusion · collaterals · plasticity</span></button><button class="system" onclick="openSys('blood')"><b>Platelets / thrombosis</b><span>P2Y12 · COX-1/TXA₂ · fibrinolysis</span></button><button class="system" onclick="openSys('heart')"><b>Heart / vessels</b><span>BP · HR · resistance · endothelium</span></button><button class="system" onclick="openSys('liver')"><b>Liver / lipids</b><span>TG · LDL · ALT</span></button><button class="system" onclick="openSys('gut')"><b>Gut / food chemistry</b><span>substrates · SCFA · metabolic signals</span></button><button class="system" onclick="openSys('kidney')"><b>Kidney / electrolytes</b><span>creatinine · eGFR · potassium</span></button><button class="system" onclick="openSys('motor')"><b>Movement / recovery</b><span>grip · strength · gait · dexterity</span></button><button class="system" onclick="openSys('thyroid')"><b>Thyroid / metabolic context</b><span>TSH · free T4</span></button></div></div></section>
<section id="meds" class="section"><div class="hero"><div class="ey">THE COMPARATIVE MEDICINE CHART</div><h2>For every medicine: desired effect → mechanism → collateral burden → ILMB target.</h2><p class="muted">This is the central treatment view. ILMB does not pretend that food or movement blocks the same receptor. It asks whether the same required physiological result can be reached by another measurable route with lower avoidable burden.</p></div><div class="legend"><span>Pharma effect</span><span>How it works</span><span>Important side-effect pathway</span><span>ILMB target</span><span>What proves the result</span></div><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare"><thead><tr><th>Medicine</th><th>Desired effect</th><th>How it achieves it</th><th>Important collateral / side-effect path</th><th>ILMB target</th><th>What must be measured</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Clopidogrel 75 mg</strong><span class="small muted">Clopilet</span></td><td>Platelet aggregation ↓</td><td>P2Y12 inhibition → ADP signalling ↓</td><td class="harm">The same platelet inhibition that reduces thrombosis also reduces normal hemostasis, so bleeding/bruising can increase; rare TTP is also reported.</td><td class="ilmb">Reduce pathological thrombosis through endothelium, metabolic load, fibrinolysis and vascular state <b>without deliberately disabling P2Y12</b>, if equivalent protection can be demonstrated.</td><td class="measure">Clinical recurrence/bleeding; platelet response testing only if specifically indicated; vascular/endothelial/metabolic state.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Aspirin 150 mg</strong><span class="small muted">Ecosprin</span></td><td>TXA₂-mediated platelet aggregation ↓</td><td>COX-1 inhibition → thromboxane ↓</td><td class="harm">Platelet hemostasis ↓; aspirin also carries GI bleeding/irritation risk.</td><td class="ilmb">Reduce net thrombosis through alternate vascular/endothelial/fibrinolytic paths while avoiding unnecessary GI/bleeding burden.</td><td class="measure">Bleeding/GI symptoms; platelet count/Hb where relevant; vascular and thrombosis-state outputs.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Amlodipine 5 mg</strong><span class="small muted">Amlodac AT component</span></td><td>BP / vascular resistance ↓</td><td>L-type Ca²⁺ blockade → arterial relaxation</td><td class="harm">Vasodilation can also cause edema, dizziness and hypotension; edema is a common dose-related adverse effect.</td><td class="ilmb">Lower vascular resistance/BP through physiologic endothelial/autonomic conditioning without pharmacologic Ca-channel blockade.</td><td class="measure">Seated BP, symptoms, edema, orthostatic response if needed.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Atenolol 50 mg</strong><span class="small muted">Amlodac AT component</span></td><td>HR and cardiac workload ↓</td><td>β1 blockade</td><td class="harm">Can cause bradycardia, hypotension, fatigue, tiredness, dizziness and suppress exercise HR response.</td><td class="ilmb">Lower resting cardiac demand through improved conditioning/autonomic regulation without blocking β1.</td><td class="measure">Resting HR, BP, workload/RPE, fatigue/dizziness; avoid generic age-based target HR while on atenolol.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Atorvastatin 40 mg</strong><span class="small muted">Lipvas 40</span></td><td>LDL/apoB ↓</td><td>HMG-CoA reductase inhibition → LDL clearance ↑</td><td class="harm">Can cause muscle injury/myopathy and liver-enzyme abnormalities; rare rhabdomyolysis is documented.</td><td class="ilmb">Lower LDL/apoB through intestinal, hepatic-substrate and metabolic routes without directly inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase.</td><td class="measure">LDL/non-HDL/apoB; ALT/AST; muscle symptoms, CK only when clinically relevant.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Fenofibrate 160 mg</strong><span class="small muted">Finobrate 160</span></td><td>TG-rich particles ↓</td><td>PPARα activation → TG clearance ↑</td><td class="harm">Can cause liver injury/enzyme rise, muscle injury, reversible creatinine rise and gallstones; muscle risk is higher when combined with a statin.</td><td class="ilmb">Reduce triglyceride production and increase triglyceride/fatty-acid utilisation through food chemistry, hepatic substrate control, gut-metabolic routes and muscle activity — without pharmacologic PPARα activation.</td><td class="measure">Fasting TG; ALT/AST/GGT; creatinine/eGFR; muscle symptoms.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Cod liver oil 300 mg</strong><span class="small muted">Seacap</span></td><td>Nutrient support</td><td>Depends on actual EPA/DHA/vitamin A/vitamin D content</td><td class="harm">Potential excess/interactions depend on the exact label chemistry; bleeding relevance depends on actual omega-3 exposure.</td><td class="ilmb">Treat as measured chemical input: identify exact EPA/DHA/A/D first, then compare against a demonstrated requirement.</td><td class="measure">Product back-label chemistry + relevant biomarkers.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Neurobion Forte</strong></td><td>B-vitamin cofactor support</td><td>Supplies multiple B-vitamin cofactors</td><td class="harm">Not automatically beneficial if no requirement gap exists; exact formulation and dose matter.</td><td class="ilmb">Requirement-first nutrient calculation from food + supplement chemistry rather than routine supplementation by habit.</td><td class="measure">Need-specific biomarkers; B12 already recorded 349 pg/mL.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><div class="call warn" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Critical:</b> For clopidogrel and aspirin, ILMB has not established direct receptor-level replacement. The comparison is between required body protection and alternate routes, not a claim that the molecular mechanism is equivalent.</div></section>
<section id="paths" class="section"><div class="card"><div class="ey">PHARMA EFFECT = ILMB REQUIRED EFFECT</div><h2>Compare benefit and burden separately.</h2><div class="two"><div class="card"><div class="ey">PHARMA</div><div class="formula">P = Bₚ − Hₚ</div><p class="small muted">Desired benefit minus collateral treatment burden.</p></div><div class="arrow">⇄</div><div class="card"><div class="ey">ILMB</div><div class="formula">I = Bᵢ − Hᵢ</div><p class="small muted">Required benefit by another route minus its own burden.</p></div></div><div class="call warn" style="margin-top:12px">Operational goal: <b>Bᵢ ≈ Bₚ while Hᵢ &lt; Hₚ where demonstrable</b>. Unknown equivalence remains blocked rather than invented.</div></div></section>
<section id="ilmb" class="section"><div class="card"><div class="ey">MY ILMB TREATMENT METHOD</div><h2>Step by step.</h2><div class="phase"><b>STEP 1</b><div><strong>Protect current proven treatment.</strong><p class="small muted">Continue prescribed medicines. Record real side effects rather than assuming them.</p></div><span class="badge known">NOW</span></div><div class="phase"><b>STEP 2</b><div><strong>Establish current vascular state.</strong><p class="small muted">Current right-MCA lumen/flow, collaterals and regional perfusion; document present reason and duration for dual antiplatelet therapy.</p></div><span class="badge bad">MISSING</span></div><div class="phase"><b>STEP 3</b><div><strong>Repeat unresolved measurements.</strong><p class="small muted">Fasting lipids, fasting glucose, CBC/platelets, ALT/AST/GGT, creatinine/eGFR, potassium, TSH + free T4, 25-OH-D, urinalysis + ACR.</p></div><span class="badge watch">BASELINE</span></div><div class="phase"><b>STEP 4</b><div><strong>Quantify motor function.</strong><p class="small muted">Bilateral grip, strength, 10-m walk, sit-to-stand, dexterity, balance, ROM and tone.</p></div><span class="badge watch">BASELINE</span></div><div class="phase"><b>STEP 5</b><div><strong>Capture actual food chemistry.</strong><p class="small muted">Three normal weighed days. Food grams → IFCT/USDA composition → chemicals → ChEBI → pathways.</p></div><span class="badge watch">3 DAYS</span></div><div class="phase"><b>STEP 6</b><div><strong>Build the personalised ILMB input.</strong><p class="small muted">Only body nodes shown to be abnormal get active treatment modules.</p></div><span class="badge unknown">AFTER BASELINE</span></div><div class="phase"><b>STEP 7</b><div><strong>Hold inputs stable.</strong><p class="small muted">Do not change everything at once. Delivered dose/exposure must be known.</p></div><span class="badge unknown">CONTROLLED</span></div><div class="phase"><b>STEP 8</b><div><strong>Re-measure and calculate Nilesh's response.</strong><div class="formula">k = Δ measured output ÷ delivered input</div></div><span class="badge known">PERSONAL MATH</span></div></div></section>
<section id="labs" class="section"><div class="card"><div class="ey">MY NUMBERS · LOINC BEHIND THE SCENES</div><h2>Every test has one exact identity.</h2><div class="table"><table class="basic"><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Known value</th><th>LOINC</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Triglycerides</td><td>301 mg/dL</td><td>2571-8</td><td>Metabolic / vascular thrombogenic load</td></tr><tr><td>HbA1c</td><td>5.8%</td><td>4548-4</td><td>Long-term glycemic state</td></tr><tr><td>ALT</td><td>81 U/L</td><td>1742-6</td><td>Liver safety / metabolic context</td></tr><tr><td>Creatinine</td><td>1.27 mg/dL</td><td>2160-0</td><td>Kidney safety</td></tr><tr><td>Potassium</td><td>5.3 mmol/L</td><td>2823-3</td><td>Electrolyte safety</td></tr><tr><td>Platelets</td><td>133 ×10³/µL</td><td>777-3</td><td>Antiplatelet / bleeding context</td></tr><tr><td>25-OH Vitamin D</td><td>13.9 ng/mL</td><td>62292-8</td><td>Nutrient state</td></tr><tr><td>TSH</td><td>5.386 µIU/mL</td><td>3016-3</td><td>Thyroid state</td></tr><tr><td>LDL-C</td><td>76.08 mg/dL</td><td>13457-7</td><td>Atherogenic lipid state</td></tr><tr><td>HDL-C</td><td>40 mg/dL</td><td>2085-9</td><td>Lipid state</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>
<section id="evidence" class="section"><div class="grid g2"><div class="card"><div class="ey">BACKGROUND IDENTITY LAYER</div><h2>How LOINC, ChEBI and Reactome work together.</h2><p><b>LOINC</b> tells ILMB exactly what was measured.</p><p><b>ChEBI</b> tells ILMB exactly which chemical or biochemical entity is involved.</p><p><b>Reactome</b> tells ILMB which biological reactions/pathways connect those entities.</p><div class="mini-flow"><span class="node">Patient result</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">LOINC</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">body state</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">ChEBI</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">Reactome</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">intervention</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">remeasure</span></div></div><div class="card"><div class="ey">EXAMPLES</div><h2>Patient label vs database identity.</h2><p>Triglycerides → <span class="code">LOINC 2571-8</span></p><p>Platelets → <span class="code">LOINC 777-3</span></p><p>Butyrate → <span class="code">ChEBI 17968</span></p><p>L-tryptophan → <span class="code">ChEBI 16828</span></p><p>Nitric oxide → <span class="code">ChEBI 16480</span></p><p>Arachidonic acid → <span class="code">ChEBI 15843</span></p></div></div><div class="card" style="margin-top:14px"><div class="ey">UNKNOWN ≠ ZERO</div><h2>Blocked claims stay visible.</h2><div class="pillrow"><span class="pill">current MCA perfusion</span><span class="pill">direct non-drug P2Y12 equivalence</span><span class="pill">direct non-drug COX-1/TXA₂ equivalence</span><span class="pill">current 3-day food chemistry</span><span class="pill">current motor vector</span><span class="pill">exact cod-liver-oil composition</span><span class="pill">patient-specific response coefficients</span></div></div></section>
</main></div><div id="drawer" class="drawer"><button class="close" onclick="closeDrawer()">×</button><div id="drawerContent"></div></div><script>
const systems={brain:['Brain / right MCA','Known: right fronto-parieto-temporal ischemic injury with persistent severe right-MCA disease on later MRA. Missing: current lumen, collateral reserve and tissue perfusion.'],blood:['Platelets / thrombosis','Clopidogrel directly changes P2Y12 signaling; aspirin directly changes COX-1/TXA₂. ILMB can improve background thrombogenic state but direct receptor equivalence is not established.'],heart:['Heart / vessels','Track BP, resting HR, vascular resistance and endothelial function. Atenolol changes how exercise HR should be interpreted.'],liver:['Liver / lipids','TG 301 mg/dL and ALT 81 U/L make lipid production/clearance and liver safety part of the same picture.'],gut:['Gut / food chemistry','Food grams are converted to nutrients/chemicals; those identities can map to ChEBI and pathway relevance when governed mappings exist.'],kidney:['Kidney / electrolytes','Creatinine 1.27, eGFR 72 and potassium 5.3 alter the safety boundary for diet, supplements and medicines.'],motor:['Movement / recovery','Current function needs numbers: grip, strength, gait, sit-to-stand, dexterity, balance, ROM and tone.'],thyroid:['Thyroid / metabolic context','TSH 5.386 should be interpreted with repeat TSH and free T4; it is context, not automatically a stroke cause.']};
const $=id=>document.getElementById(id);document.querySelectorAll('nav button[data-tab]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>go(b.dataset.tab));document.querySelectorAll('[data-go]').forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>go(x.dataset.go));function go(id){document.querySelectorAll('.section').forEach(s=>s.classList.remove('active'));document.querySelectorAll('nav button').forEach(b=>b.classList.remove('active'));$(id).classList.add('active');document.querySelector(`nav button[data-tab="${id}"]`)?.classList.add('active');scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}function openSys(k){$('drawerContent').innerHTML=`<div class="ey">MY BODY</div><h2>${systems[k][0]}</h2><p>${systems[k][1]}</p>`;$('drawer').classList.add('open')}function closeDrawer(){$('drawer').classList.remove('open')}
</script></body></html>